+   an invalid port is specified in the URL.  Port validation occurs when
+   the :attr:`port` attribute is accessed, not when the URL is parsed or
+   the result object is constructed.  A valid port must be an integer in
+   the range 0 through 65535 (inclusive).  If the port portion of the
+   netloc is absent or empty, :attr:`port` returns :const:`None`.
+
+   The following examples illustrate port boundary and error behavior.
+   The URL is parsed successfully in every case; the :exc:`ValueError`
+   is raised only when ``.port`` is evaluated.  The same behavior applies
+   to both :func:`urlsplit` and :func:`urlparse`.
+
+   .. doctest::
+      :options: +NORMALIZE_WHITESPACE
+
+      >>> from urllib.parse import urlsplit
+      >>> p = urlsplit("http://example.com:0/")
+      >>> p.port
+      0
+      >>> p = urlsplit("http://example.com:65535/")
+      >>> p.port
+      65535
+      >>> p = urlsplit("http://example.com:")
+      >>> p.port
+      >>> p = urlsplit("http://example.com/")
+      >>> p.port
+
+   Ports outside the valid range, or that cannot be interpreted as an
+   ASCII decimal integer, raise :exc:`ValueError` at attribute-access
+   time:
+
+   .. doctest::
+      :options: +NORMALIZE_WHITESPACE
+
+      >>> p = urlsplit("http://example.com:65536/")
+      >>> p.port
